pics have received relatively high attention: Deployment flexibility: Proposals from Document R3-256571: The 6G RAN architecture shall allow flexible deployment that enables introduction of new 6G services in flexible manner during life cycle of 6G RAN with minimum or not impacting already deployed interface functions/services. Proposals from Document R3-256945: Base the 6G RAN design on the concept of modularity at function, protocol and interface procedure level, with the objective to support flexible distribution of 6G RAN processes. Proposals from Document R3-256904 and R3-256615: The 6G RAN architecture shall allow for deployment flexibility e.g. to host relevant RAN, CN and application functions close together at the edges of the network. Proposals from Document R3-256622: Migration from NR to 6GR will require procedure-level support for mobility, interoperability, and deployment flexibility across multiple scenarios. In addition, further migration options are expected to be studied in more detail during the 6G Radio SI. In 5G, the need to support diverse services such as low-latency communications means that deployment flexibility is an important requirement for architecture design, In 6G, with the emergence of various new services, such demands have become even more important for 6G architecture design. Therefore, it is proposed that, RAN3 could agree the following principles and requirements： - The 6G RAN architecture shall allow for deployment flexibility, e.g, in case a specific service or function is deployed for a particular objective, it will not affect or will minimally affect the existing interface function or services. 2）RAN Split architecture Proposals from Document R3-256571: The 6G RAN Architecture shall support both upper layer split and non-split RAN NF deployment scenarios. Proposals from Document R3-256615: The RAN architecture shall allow for CU-DU splitting. Proposals from Document R3-256687: 6GR architecture should continue support for dis-aggregated architecture. Proposals from Document R3-256622: The 6G RAN architecture should preserve the principle of functional separation established in NR, while reassessing the necessity of CP–UP split and confirming whether existing coordination mechanisms remain adequate for distributed deployments. Proposals from Document R3-256945: Handling of UE-associated functionalities such as UE configurations, UE state management, UE capability handling etc. shall be controlled by a single logical RAN entity. Proposals from Document R3-256539: This means the 6G RAN will be a single logical standardized entity comprising all RAN protocol stacks and functions. For detailed technical discussions on RAN split architecture, please refer to the topics in 10.3.2. However, as an important part of RAN architecture design, RAN3 needs to put forward corresponding principles and requirements. Similar to the relevant proposals in 5G, both split and non-split RAN deployment scenario should be supported in 6G, as the benefits of introducing the split architecture discussed in 5G remain valid. Nevertheless, considering that there are different viewpoints on this technical topic, the corresponding TP can be kept as FFS. Subsequently, the description can be revised again according to the final result of 10.3.2. Therefore, it is proposed that RAN3 could agree the following principles and requirements: - The RAN architecture shall allow both split and non-split RAN deployment scenarios. [FFS] 3）Independent Evolvement of RAN and CN Proposals from Document R3-256852: The 6G RAN architecture shall allow for the RAN and the CN to evolve independently. Proposals from Document R3-256615: The RAN architecture shall allow for the RAN and the CN to evolve independently. Proposals f

